You are one of 6 parallel sub-testers reviewing the FINAL commit (20 of 20) of the SFCC-11 Cart epic on branch `feature/SFCC-11_cart` in /Users/marcel.a.n.navarro/Dev/zimmermann-pwa (already checked out — do not switch branches, do not commit, do not push, do not fix anything you find). Other agents cover: mechanical gates, the Storybook no-op mutation test, focus-return mechanics/accessibility, structure/imports/DRY, and AC-by-AC + modal-regression rendering. Stay in your lane: **the auto-close-on-route-change judgment call, and general React hook-logic correctness of the new `overrides/app/hooks/useCartDrawer.ts` file.**

Read `overrides/app/hooks/useCartDrawer.ts` in full first (202 lines, every design decision is explained in its own comments) — it is the entire subject of most of your review.

## Part 1 — Verify the disclosed judgment call

`useCartDrawerState()` contains:
```ts
const { pathname } = useLocation();
useEffect(() => {
  if (isOpen) { onClose(); }
}, [pathname]);
```
The file's own comment claims this is load-bearing, not cosmetic: `ZimmCartButtonGroup`'s Checkout button (`overrides/app/components/ui-kits/molecules/zimmCartButtonGroup/index.tsx`) navigates via `navigate('/checkout')` with no `onClose()` call of its own, and `ZimmDrawer`'s overlay isn't click-through — so without this effect, clicking Checkout from inside the open drawer would navigate underneath a still-open, non-dismissible overlay.

1. Read `overrides/app/components/ui-kits/molecules/zimmCartButtonGroup/index.tsx`'s actual `handleCheckoutClick` yourself (independently — don't just take this prompt's word) and confirm: does it really call `navigate('/checkout')` (via `useNavigation()`) with no `onClose`/close-callback anywhere in its own body or props? Does `ZimmCartButtonGroup` even receive an `onClose` prop it could call (check its full props type)? Confirm whether the component has ANY mechanism at all to close the drawer other than this hook-level effect.
2. Confirm `ZimmDrawer`'s overlay is genuinely non-click-through — read `overrides/app/components/ui-kits/atoms/zimmDrawer/index.tsx` (and its recipe if relevant) for the overlay's pointer-events/z-index behaviour, don't just accept the claim.
3. Is the hook-level `[pathname]` effect really the ONLY thing preventing the stuck-overlay scenario, or does something else already handle it (e.g. does `ZimmDrawer`/`CartDrawerContainer` itself already close on navigation some other way, making this redundant)? Report CONFIRMED or PLAUSIBLE.

## Part 2 — A specific redundancy you must investigate, not assume either way

The coordinating Tester noticed something and needs it independently checked: `overrides/app/components/product-view/index.tsx` ALSO has its own, PRE-EXISTING `useEffect` (unchanged in shape by this diff, just its target renamed):
```ts
useEffect(() => {
  if (isCartDrawerOpen) {
    onCartDrawerClose();
  }
}, [location.pathname]);
```
(`location` there is from `useLocation()` imported from `react-router-dom` — same package/hook as `_app`'s and the new hook's own `useLocation()` call). Before this diff, this effect closed the OLD `AddToCartModal` on route change (this pattern is a straight port of base's own `use-add-to-cart-modal.js`'s `[pathname]`-keyed effect) — this diff just swapped its target from `isAddToCartModalOpen`/`onAddToCartModalClose` to `isCartDrawerOpen`/`onCartDrawerClose`.

Now BOTH `useCartDrawerState()` (called once, in `_app`) and `product-view`'s own effect close the SAME shared drawer state on the SAME kind of event (`pathname`/`location.pathname` change), independently.

Investigate and answer definitively:
1. Are `_app`'s `useLocation()` and `product-view`'s `useLocation()` guaranteed to observe the same pathname change in the same commit (both are descendants of the same Router, both call the same react-router-dom hook)? Is there any realistic scenario (route change timing, effect ordering, product-view being unmounted/remounted independently, product-view rendering in a context `_app` doesn't wrap, etc.) where one of these two effects would fire and the other wouldn't, making them NOT fully redundant? Think concretely about the actual component tree (`_app` renders the page which eventually renders `product-view` on the PDP) — is `product-view` always inside `_app`'s subtree whenever it's rendered? Check where product-view is used (`overrides/app/pages/product-detail/`) to confirm it's always a descendant of `_app`.
2. If you conclude they ARE fully redundant (product-view's own effect no longer does anything `_app`'s hook-level effect doesn't already do), this is a real finding: dead/duplicate logic left over from the migration from `AddToCartModal` to `CartDrawer`, that should have been removed from `product-view` once the shared hook took over the same responsibility globally. State it as a finding with file:line, severity (this is a "should-fix" quality/DRY issue, not a functional break, since both effects independently calling `onClose()` on an already-closing/closed drawer is harmless — but confirm that harmlessness yourself: does calling `onClose()` twice, or calling it when already closed, cause any double-render, warning, or issue? Check `useDisclosure()`'s behavior or reason about it) — but if you find a real behavioral difference between the two effects (not just "one is technically redundant but harmless"), report that instead, precisely.

## Part 3 — React logic correctness checklist on the new hook file

1. `useEffect` dependency arrays: is `[pathname]` really deliberately excluding `isOpen`/`onClose` (the file's own comment says so — "isOpen/onClose are read for their current value, not to decide when the effect re-runs") — does omitting them cause any stale-closure bug? Since `onClose` is `useDisclosure()`'s own returned function (from Chakra), is it referentially stable across renders (Chakra typically memoizes these) such that omitting it from deps is safe? Check Chakra's `useDisclosure` implementation if accessible in node_modules, or reason from its typical documented behavior.
2. The `triggerRef` cast: `const triggerRef = useRef<HTMLElement>(null as unknown as HTMLElement);` — the comment claims this matches precedent at `overrides/app/hooks/useWishlistAuthDrawer.ts:140`. Read that file around that line yourself and confirm the precedent is real and the reasoning (wanting `MutableRefObject` instead of the read-only `RefObject` a plain `useRef<HTMLElement>(null)` would infer) is sound TypeScript reasoning, not just asserted.
3. `onOpen`'s trigger-capture logic: `if (trigger) triggerRef.current = trigger; onDisclosureOpen();` — is there any race/ordering issue between setting the ref (a mutation, synchronous, not batched) and calling `onDisclosureOpen()` (a state setter, triggers a re-render)? Reason about whether a consumer reading `triggerRef.current` during the SAME render pass that opens the drawer could see a stale value — walk through: does anything actually read `triggerRef.current` synchronously right after `onOpen()` is called, before the re-render? (Check `_app`'s and `product-view`'s call sites — they call `onOpen`/`onCartDrawerOpen` and then don't immediately read `triggerRef`, so likely fine — confirm.)
4. Is there a plausible edge case this file's own 15 tests DON'T cover? (e.g., calling `onOpen` twice in a row with two different triggers without an intervening `onClose` — the tests show this only tested WITH an intervening `onClose`. Does skipping the intervening close still correctly overwrite `triggerRef.current`, per the code's own logic? Read the code, not just the tests, to answer — if the code has no such gate, the answer should be a straightforward "yes, unconditional overwrite," but confirm and note if the test suite has a coverage gap here.)

Report format: file:line for every finding, CONFIRMED (you traced the actual code/tests) or PLAUSIBLE (reasoned, not fully traced), 🔴/🟡/🔵, and UNIFORM/ONE-OFF. If Parts 1-3 all come back clean, say so plainly — don't pad.

